# Test: a session of the running ze shows the help an external plugin's command YANG declares
#
# Question: when an external plugin brings a -cmd module whose nodes declare a
# ze:help summary and a description, and states DIFFERENT texts for the same
# commands at Stage 1, does a session of the running ze show the YANG texts
# (the summary under the completion menu, the explanation `?` draws) and does
# `system command help` answer them, never the Stage 1 ones?
# Why not a unit test: TestPluginCommandTakesTheHelpItsYANGNodeDeclares proves
# the precedence over two maps. Only a daemon shows the session model it hosts
# built from the module set boot published, the plugin's Stage 1 registration
# overlaid on it, and the registry answer `system command help` reads.
#
# What the screen and the answer show, read from the producing code:
#   - Tab on `show acme-monitor ` opens the menu with status selected, and the
#     row under it holds status's summary (Model.warningText); a second Tab
#     selects target and the row holds its summary.
#   - `?` after Tab draws the description of status
#     (Model.revealCandidateExplanation, TreeCompleter.Explain).
#   - `system command help` answers the registry entry (lookupCommandHelp),
#     whose texts registerProcessCommands takes from the YANG where the node
#     declares them (commandModelHelp.texts).
#   - the Stage 1 texts (externalyang.DeclaredSummary, DeclaredExplanation)
#     appear on none of them.
#
# VALIDATES: spec-plugin-external-yang-at-startup AC-30: help on the plugin's
#   command in a session; where the YANG and the Stage 1 declaration both give
#   help, the YANG text is shown.
# PREVENTS: an external plugin's Stage 1 summary shown where its own command
#   module declares one.
# Not proven here: a session surface that shows the argument leaf's own ze:help
#   ("Name of the target."). No session surface shows an argument leaf's help
#   for any command, compiled-in included: TreeCompleter.matchChildren offers a
#   string leaf by its name with no description. The API half is
#   external-yang-command-api-parameters.ci.
#
# Method: the compiled fixture
# (internal/test/fixture/ui_fixture_external_yang_command_help.go) starts a
# daemon with the external-yang plugin in its `commands` variant, waits until
# the plugin answers its command, and drives a plain ssh client under a
# pseudo-terminal on the operational prompt.
#
# The timeout is DECLARED because this file starts a daemon and drives three
# interactive terminal sessions, each with a settling window.
option=timeout:value=120s

cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=le test fixture ui/external-yang-command-help $PORT
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stdout:contains=OK: the session showed the YANG summaries of status and target and the description of status, and system command help answered the YANG texts
reject=stdout:pattern=ZE-OBSERVER-FAIL
